00:44:54,408 --> 00:45:00,308
E-mini S&P 500, German Bunds, WTI Oil and the Eurodollar FX.

713
00:45:01,008 --> 00:45:04,008
And then lastly, set up your demo account.

714
00:45:04,508 --> 00:45:08,668
Make sure you spend some time doing all of this as when

715
00:45:08,668 --> 00:45:11,094
we start module 2, you're going to be

716
00:45:11,094 --> 00:45:14,214
expected to have understood everything you've learned in module 1.

717
00:45:15,408 --> 00:45:16,008
Good luck.

718
00:45:17,008 --> 00:45:17,808
Thank you very much.

719
00:45:20,956 --> 00:45:21,556
Thank you.

720
00:45:24,356 --> 00:45:26,192
Well, thank you, President

721
00:45:26,192 --> 00:45:28,028
Powers, Provost Fenves, Deans,

722
00:45:28,028 --> 00:45:29,864
members of the faculty,

723
00:45:29,864 --> 00:45:31,700
family and friends, and

724
00:45:31,700 --> 00:45:33,995
most importantly, the class of 2014.

725
00:45:37,656 --> 00:45:40,656
It is indeed an honor for me to be here tonight.

726
00:45:41,256 --> 00:45:46,156
It's been almost 37 years to the day that I graduated from UT.

727
00:45:46,356 --> 00:45:48,456
I remember a lot of things about that day.

728
00:45:49,656 --> 00:45:53,456
I remember I had a throbbing headache from a party the night before.

729
00:45:58,568 --> 00:46:01,368
I remember I had a serious girlfriend, who I later married.

730
00:46:03,068 --> 00:46:04,568
That's important to remember, by the way.

731
00:46:05,468 --> 00:46:07,868
And I remember I was getting commissioned in the Navy that day.

732
00:46:08,168 --> 00:46:11,248
But of all the things I remember, I don't have

733
00:46:11,248 --> 00:46:13,712
a clue who the commencement speaker was, and

734
00:46:13,712 --> 00:46:15,868
I certainly don't remember anything they said.

735
00:46:16,568 --> 00:46:18,567
So acknowledging that fact, if I can't

736
00:46:18,567 --> 00:46:20,851
make this commencement speech memorable, I will at

737
00:46:20,851 --> 00:46:22,564
least try to make it short.

738
00:46:24,568 --> 00:46:28,168
So the university's slogan is, what starts here changes the world.

739
00:46:28,168 --> 00:46:33,368
Well, I've got to admit, I kind of like it. What starts here changes the world.

740
00:46:33,368 --> 00:46:39,068
Tonight there are almost 8,000 students, or there are more than 8,000 students graduated from UT.

741
00:46:39,868 --> 00:46:43,668
So that great paragon of analytical rigor, ask.com,

742
00:46:44,468 --> 00:46:49,268
says that the average American will meet 10,000 people in their lifetime.

743
00:46:50,168 --> 00:46:51,668
10,000 people, that's a lot of folks.

744
00:46:52,468 --> 00:46:56,412
But if every one of you changed the lives of just 10

745
00:46:56,412 --> 00:46:59,998
people, and each one of those people changed the lives

746
00:46:59,998 --> 00:47:01,791
of another 10 people, and another 10,

747
00:47:03,568 --> 00:47:12,668
Then in five generations, 125 years, the class of 2014 will have changed the lives of 800 million people.

748
00:47:13,468 --> 00:47:14,968
800 million people.

749
00:47:16,268 --> 00:47:16,868
Think about it.

750
00:47:18,168 --> 00:47:20,168
Over twice the population of the United States.

751
00:47:20,168 --> 00:47:23,968
Go one more generation and you can change the entire population of the United States.

752
00:47:26,368 --> 00:47:32,168
If you think it's hard to change the lives of 10 people, change their lives forever, you're wrong.

753
00:47:32,568 --> 00:47:34,968
I saw it happen every day in Iraq and Afghanistan.

754
00:47:35,768 --> 00:47:39,328
A young Army officer makes a decision to go left instead of

755
00:47:39,328 --> 00:47:44,371
right down a road in Baghdad, and the 10 soldiers with him are saved from a close-in ambush.

756
00:47:45,768 --> 00:47:50,062
In Kandahar Province, Afghanistan, a non-commissioned officer from the female

757
00:47:50,062 --> 00:47:54,356
engagement team senses that something isn't right and directs the

758
00:47:54,356 --> 00:47:58,650
infantry platoon away from a 500-pound IED, saving the lives

759
00:47:58,650 --> 00:48:00,367
of a dozen soldiers.

760
00:48:01,768 --> 00:48:06,268
But if you think about it, not only were those soldiers saved by the decisions of one person,

761
00:48:07,268 --> 00:48:10,268
But their children were saved, and their children's children.

762
00:48:10,868 --> 00:48:14,668
Generations were saved by one decision, one person.

763
00:48:15,868 --> 00:48:19,168
But changing the world can happen anywhere, and anyone can do it.

764
00:48:19,868 --> 00:48:22,268
So what starts here can indeed change the world.

765
00:48:22,368 --> 00:48:26,468
But the question is, what will the world look like after you change it?

766
00:48:27,468 --> 00:48:29,468
Well, I'm confident that it will look much, much better.

767
00:48:30,368 --> 00:48:34,018
But if you'll humor this old sailor for just a moment, I have a few suggestions that

768
00:48:34,018 --> 00:48:36,165
may help you on your way to a better world.

769
00:48:36,668 --> 00:48:40,095
And while these lessons were learned during my time in the military,

770
00:48:40,095 --> 00:48:44,665
I can assure you that it matters not whether you ever served a day in uniform.

771
00:48:45,168 --> 00:48:50,668
It matters not your gender. Your ethnic or religious background, your orientation, or your social status.

772
00:48:51,268 --> 00:48:53,868
Our struggles in this world are similar, and the lessons

773
00:48:53,968 --> 00:48:57,444
to overcome those struggles and to move forward, changing ourselves

774
00:48:57,444 --> 00:49:01,267
and changing the world around us will apply equally to all.

775
00:49:02,568 --> 00:49:05,002
I've been a Navy SEAL for 36 years, but it

776
00:49:05,002 --> 00:49:08,518
all began when I left UT for basic SEAL training in Coronado, California.

777
00:49:10,168 --> 00:49:14,768
Basic SEAL training is six months of long, torturous runs in the soft sand.

778
00:49:15,368 --> 00:49:17,454
Midnight swims in the cold water

779
00:49:17,454 --> 00:49:19,540
off San Diego, obstacle courses, unending

780
00:49:19,540 --> 00:49:23,364
calisthenics, days without sleep, and always being cold, wet, and miserable.

781
00:49:23,668 --> 00:49:29,153
It is six months of being constantly harassed by professionally trained warriors

782
00:49:29,153 --> 00:49:33,724
who seek to find the weak of mind and body

783
00:49:33,724 --> 00:49:36,466
and eliminate them from ever becoming.

784
00:49:36,768 --> 00:49:37,568
A Navy SEAL.

785
00:49:38,568 --> 00:49:43,368
But the training also seeks to find those students who can lead

786
00:49:43,368 --> 00:49:47,368
in an environment of constant stress, chaos, failure, and hardships.

787
00:49:48,168 --> 00:49:52,868
To me, basic SEAL training was a lifetime of challenges crammed into six months.

788
00:49:53,568 --> 00:49:56,335
So here are the ten lessons I learned from basic SEAL training

789
00:49:56,335 --> 00:49:59,564
that hopefully will be of value to you as you move forward in life.

790
00:50:01,268 --> 00:50:05,055
Every morning in SEAL training, my instructors, who at the time were

791
00:50:05,055 --> 00:50:07,580
all Vietnam veterans, would show up in my

792
00:50:07,580 --> 00:50:11,367
barracks room, and the first thing they'd do was inspect my bed.

793
00:50:11,968 --> 00:50:15,156
If you did it right, the corners would be square, the covers

794
00:50:15,156 --> 00:50:17,281
would be pulled tight, the pillow centered just

795
00:50:17,281 --> 00:50:21,266
under the headboard, and the extra blanket folded neatly at the foot of the rack.

796
00:50:22,168 --> 00:50:29,668
It was a simple task. mundane at best, but every morning we were required to make our bed to perfection.

797
00:50:30,468 --> 00:50:32,484
It seemed a little ridiculous at the

798
00:50:32,484 --> 00:50:34,788
time, particularly in light of the fact that

799
00:50:34,788 --> 00:50:37,668
we were aspiring to be real warriors, tough, battle-hardened SEALs.

800
00:50:38,668 --> 00:50:43,668
But the wisdom of this simple act has been proven to me many times over.

801
00:50:44,868 --> 00:50:49,468
If you make your bed every morning, you will have accomplished the first task of the day.

802
00:50:50,368 --> 00:50:52,468
It will give you a small sense

803
00:50:52,468 --> 00:50:53,968
of pride, and it will

804
00:50:53,968 --> 00:50:55,168
encourage you to do

805
00:50:55,168 --> 00:50:56,968
another task, and another, and another.

806
00:50:57,668 --> 00:51:01,868
And by the end of the day, that one task completed will have turned into many tasks completed.

807
00:51:02,568 --> 00:51:06,968
Making your bed will also reinforce the fact But the little things in life matter.

808
00:51:07,768 --> 00:51:12,068
If you can't do the little things right, you'll never be able to do the big things right.

809
00:51:12,968 --> 00:51:17,368
And if by chance you have a miserable day, you will come home to a bed that is made.

810
00:51:19,468 --> 00:51:20,168
That you made.

811
00:51:21,468 --> 00:51:24,568
And a made bed gives you encouragement that tomorrow will be better.

812
00:51:25,468 --> 00:51:28,868
So if you want to change the world, start off by making your bed.

813
00:51:31,460 --> 00:51:36,960
During SEAL training, the students are all broken down into boat crews.

814
00:51:37,360 --> 00:51:38,677
Each crew is seven students,

815
00:51:38,677 --> 00:51:40,521
three on each side of a small

816
00:51:40,521 --> 00:51:43,156
rubber boat, and one coxswain to help guide the dinghy.

817
00:51:44,260 --> 00:51:45,413
Every day, your boat crew

818
00:51:45,413 --> 00:51:47,027
forms up on the beach and is

819
00:51:47,027 --> 00:51:50,256
instructed to get through the surf zone and paddle several miles down the coast.

820
00:51:50,760 --> 00:51:54,435
In the winter, the surf off San Diego can get to be

821
00:51:54,435 --> 00:51:56,272
8 to 10 feet high, and it is

822
00:51:56,272 --> 00:51:59,947
exceedingly difficult to paddle through the plunging surf unless everyone digs in.

823
00:52:01,760 --> 00:52:05,660
Every paddle must be synchronized to the stroke count of the coxswain.

824
00:52:06,460 --> 00:52:08,659
Everyone must exert equal effort or the

825
00:52:08,659 --> 00:52:10,858
boat will turn against the wave and

826
00:52:10,858 --> 00:52:13,057
be unceremoniously dumped back on the beach.

827
00:52:14,060 --> 00:52:17,760
For the boat to make it to its destination, everyone must paddle.

828
00:52:18,960 --> 00:52:20,360
You can't change the world alone.

829
00:52:21,260 --> 00:52:22,160
You will need some help.

830
00:52:22,860 --> 00:52:26,940
And to truly get from your starting point to your destination takes

831
00:52:26,940 --> 00:52:31,360
friends, colleagues, the goodwill of strangers, and a strong coxswain to guide you.

832
00:52:32,060 --> 00:52:35,160
If you want to change the world, find someone to help you paddle.

833
00:52:37,332 --> 00:52:44,032
Over a few weeks of difficult training, my SEAL class, which started with 150 men, was down to just 42.

834
00:52:44,832 --> 00:52:46,932
There were now six boat crews of seven men each.

835
00:52:48,032 --> 00:52:50,155
I was in the boat with the tall

836
00:52:50,155 --> 00:52:52,013
guys, but the best boat crew we

837
00:52:52,013 --> 00:52:55,729
had was made up of the little guys, the munchkin crew we called them.

838
00:52:56,532 --> 00:52:58,032
No one was over 5'5".

839
00:52:59,332 --> 00:53:03,420
The Munchkin boat crew had one American Indian, one African American, one

840
00:53:03,420 --> 00:53:05,464
Polish American, one Greek American, one

841
00:53:05,464 --> 00:53:08,530
Italian American, and two tough kids from the Midwest.

842
00:53:09,332 --> 00:53:13,932
They out-paddled, out-ran, and out-swam all the other boat crews.

843
00:53:14,332 --> 00:53:18,632
The big men in the other boat crews would always make good-natured fun.

844
00:53:19,332 --> 00:53:25,832
Of the tiny little flippers the munchkins put on their tiny little feet prior to every swim.

845
00:53:27,132 --> 00:53:30,404
But somehow these little guys from every corner of the nation and

846
00:53:30,404 --> 00:53:32,585
the world always had the last laugh, swimming

847
00:53:32,585 --> 00:53:36,130
faster than everyone and reaching the shore long before the rest of us.

848
00:53:37,532 --> 00:53:39,032
SEAL training was a great equalizer.

849
00:53:39,832 --> 00:53:41,688
Nothing mattered but your will to

850
00:53:41,688 --> 00:53:43,544
succeed, not your color, not your

851
00:53:43,544 --> 00:53:44,781
ethnic background, not your

852
00:53:44,781 --> 00:53:46,327
education, not your social status.

853
00:53:47,232 --> 00:53:49,926
If you want to change the world, measure a person

854
00:53:49,926 --> 00:53:51,542
by the size of their heart,

855
00:53:51,542 --> 00:53:53,428
not by the size of their flippers.

856
00:53:55,232 --> 00:54:02,132
Several times a week, the instructors would line up the class and do a uniform inspection. It was exceptionally thorough.

857
00:54:02,932 --> 00:54:09,532
Your hat had to be perfectly starched, your uniform immaculate. pressed, your belt buckle shiny and void of any smudges.

858
00:54:10,732 --> 00:54:13,792
But it seemed that no matter how much effort you put into

859
00:54:13,792 --> 00:54:15,832
starching your hat or pressing your uniform or

860
00:54:15,832 --> 00:54:18,127
polishing your belt buckle, it just wasn't good enough.

861
00:54:18,932 --> 00:54:20,932
The instructors would find something wrong.

862
00:54:21,832 --> 00:54:24,732
For failing uniform inspection, the student had to run

863
00:54:25,532 --> 00:54:27,872
fully clothed into the surf zone, then wet

864
00:54:27,872 --> 00:54:30,212
from head to toe, roll around on the

865
00:54:30,212 --> 00:54:33,430
beach until every part of your body was covered with sand.

866
00:54:34,232 --> 00:54:36,632
The effect was known as a sugar cookie.

867
00:54:38,772 --> 00:54:42,372
You stayed in the uniform the rest of the day, cold, wet, and sandy.

868
00:54:43,472 --> 00:54:46,599
There were many a student who just couldn't accept the fact that

869
00:54:46,599 --> 00:54:48,683
all their efforts were in vain, that no

870
00:54:48,683 --> 00:54:52,070
matter how hard they tried to get the uniform right, it went unappreciated.

871
00:54:52,772 --> 00:54:55,172
Those students didn't make it through training.

872
00:54:55,172 --> 00:54:58,572
Those students didn't understand the purpose of the drill.

873
00:54:59,372 --> 00:55:00,898
You were never going to succeed.

874
00:55:00,898 --> 00:55:02,424
You were never going to have

875
00:55:02,424 --> 00:55:04,968
a perfect uniform. The instructors weren't going to allow it.

876
00:55:06,072 --> 00:55:12,172
Sometimes, no matter how well you prepare or how well you perform, you still end up as a sugar cookie.

877
00:55:13,072 --> 00:55:14,872
It's just the way life is sometimes.

878
00:55:15,772 --> 00:55:20,572
If you want to change the world, get over being a sugar cookie and keep moving forward.

879
00:55:22,072 --> 00:55:24,629
Every day during training, you were challenged

880
00:55:24,629 --> 00:55:27,186
with multiple physical events, long runs, long

881
00:55:27,186 --> 00:55:29,378
swims, obstacle courses, hours of calisthenics,

882
00:55:29,378 --> 00:55:31,570
something designed to test your mettle.

883
00:55:32,072 --> 00:55:34,672
Every event had standards, times you had to meet.

884
00:55:34,672 --> 00:55:39,472
If you failed to meet those times, those standards, your name was posted on a list.

885
00:55:40,072 --> 00:55:45,272
And at the end of the day, those on the list were invited to a circus.

886
00:55:45,872 --> 00:55:47,798
A circus was two hours of additional calisthenics

887
00:55:47,798 --> 00:55:49,483
designed to wear you down, to break

888
00:55:49,483 --> 00:55:51,168
your spirit, to force you to quit.

889
00:55:51,772 --> 00:55:53,672
No one wanted a circus.

890
00:55:54,372 --> 00:55:57,272
A circus meant that for that day, you didn't measure up.

891
00:55:57,772 --> 00:55:59,272
A circus meant more fatigue, and

892
00:55:59,272 --> 00:56:01,022
more fatigue meant that the following day

893
00:56:01,022 --> 00:56:03,272
would be more difficult, and more circuses were likely.

894
00:56:04,472 --> 00:56:10,572
But at some time during SEAL training, everyone, Everyone made the circus list.

895
00:56:11,872 --> 00:56:16,272
But an interesting thing happened to those who were constantly on the list.

896
00:56:17,172 --> 00:56:23,672
Over time, those students who did two hours of extra calisthenics got stronger and stronger.

897
00:56:24,572 --> 00:56:27,572
The pain of the circuses built inner strength and physical resiliency.

898
00:56:29,272 --> 00:56:30,672
Life is filled with circuses.

899
00:56:31,772 --> 00:56:34,672
You will fail. You will likely fail often.

900
00:56:34,872 --> 00:56:35,772
It will be painful.

901
00:56:36,472 --> 00:56:37,672
It will be discouraging.

902
00:56:38,072 --> 00:56:40,772
At times, it will test you to your very core.

903
00:56:41,772 --> 00:56:45,872
But if you want to change the world, don't be afraid of the circuses.

904
00:56:47,372 --> 00:56:50,472
At least twice a week, the trainees were required to run the obstacle course.

905
00:56:51,872 --> 00:56:53,048
The obstacle course contained 25

906
00:56:53,048 --> 00:56:55,106
obstacles, including a 10-foot wall, a 30-foot

907
00:56:55,106 --> 00:56:58,046
cargo net, a barbed wire crawl, to name a few.

908
00:56:58,972 --> 00:57:02,072
But the most challenging obstacle was the slide for life.

909
00:57:02,772 --> 00:57:07,272
It had a three-level, 30-foot tower at one end and a one-level tower at the other.

910
00:57:07,972 --> 00:57:10,072
In between was a 200-foot-long rope.

911
00:57:10,872 --> 00:57:13,099
You had to climb the three-tiered tower,

912
00:57:13,099 --> 00:57:15,326
and once at the top, you grabbed

913
00:57:15,326 --> 00:57:17,235
the rope, swung underneath the rope,

914
00:57:17,235 --> 00:57:21,371
and pulled yourself hand over hand until you got to the other end.

915
00:57:21,872 --> 00:57:26,372
The record for the obstacle course had stood for years when my class began in 1977.

916
00:57:26,872 --> 00:57:28,072
The record seemed unbeatable.

917
00:57:28,972 --> 00:57:34,372
Until one day a student decided to go down the slide for life head first.

918
00:57:35,272 --> 00:57:37,232
Instead of swinging his body underneath the

919
00:57:37,232 --> 00:57:39,192
rope, and inching his way down, he

920
00:57:39,192 --> 00:57:42,272
bravely mounted the top of the rope and thrust himself forward.

921
00:57:42,572 --> 00:57:46,772
It was a dangerous move, seemingly foolish and fraught with risk.

922
00:57:47,272 --> 00:57:49,272
Failure could mean injury and being dropped from the course.

923
00:57:49,272 --> 00:57:53,372
Without hesitation, the student slid down the rope perilously fast.

924
00:57:53,972 --> 00:57:57,172
Instead of several minutes, it only took him half that time.

925
00:57:57,872 --> 00:58:01,072
And by the end of the course, he had broken the record.

926
00:58:02,372 --> 00:58:06,872
If you want to change the world, sometimes you have to slide down the obstacles head first.

927
00:58:08,872 --> 00:58:10,738
During the land warfare phase of training, the

928
00:58:10,738 --> 00:58:14,470
students are flown out to San Clemente Island, which lies off the coast of San Diego.

929
00:58:15,472 --> 00:58:18,572
The waters off San Clemente are a breeding ground for the great white sharks.

930
00:58:19,572 --> 00:58:22,772
To pass SEAL training, there are a series of long swims that must be completed.

931
00:58:23,472 --> 00:58:24,572
One is the night swim.

932
00:58:25,372 --> 00:58:28,072
Before the swim, the instructors joyfully brief the students.

933
00:58:28,972 --> 00:58:33,572
On all the species of sharks that inhabit the waters off San Clemente.

934
00:58:34,672 --> 00:58:36,504
They assure you, however, that

935
00:58:36,504 --> 00:58:39,069
no student has ever been eaten by

936
00:58:39,069 --> 00:58:42,367
a shark, at least not that they can remember.

937
00:58:44,072 --> 00:58:49,972
But you are also taught that if a shark begins to circle your position, Stand your ground.

938
00:58:50,772 --> 00:58:51,872
Do not swim away.

939
00:58:52,772 --> 00:58:53,972
Do not act afraid.

940
00:58:54,972 --> 00:58:58,688
And if a shark, hungry for a midnight snack, darts towards you,

941
00:58:58,688 --> 00:59:01,165
then summons up all your strength and punch

942
00:59:01,165 --> 00:59:04,571
him in the snout, and he will turn and swim away.

943
00:59:06,072 --> 00:59:07,072
There are a lot of sharks in the world.

944
00:59:08,472 --> 00:59:11,972
If you hope to complete the swim, you will have to deal with them.

945
00:59:13,072 --> 00:59:14,472
